<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ullastret</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ullastret is an archaeological site in the Baix <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emporda">Empordà</a> in Catalonia, the largest known Iberian settlement in the region and the chief town of the Indigetes. Occupied from the 6th century BCE, it had stone walls with towers, temples and grain silos, and its buildings and imports show close contact with the nearby Greek colony of Emporion. It was abandoned around 200 BCE after the Roman conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ullastret.jpg" alt="Ullastret" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josep Renalias · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MurallesUllastret.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…are still being uncovered and studied: the settlement of Castellet de Banyoles was discovered in 1912, the ruins of <b>Ullastret</b> are identified with Indika, the centre of the Indigetes, and the mausoleum of Pozo Moro near Chinchilla de Monte-Aragón seems to mark the site of another large settlement.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Their largest settlement, at <b>Ullastret</b>, was a walled hill town with towers, temples and grain silos, showing strong Greek influence in its architecture and imports.</blockquote><a href="/state/indigetes">Indigetes</a></li></ul></section>
